---
intent: >
  Storage-specific vocabulary (vault, hub note, wikilink, note-as-synonym, obsidian)
  appears in the JSDoc and inline template string constants of
  obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts, which is this module's code surface, violating
  the storage-agnostic-vocabulary outcome. The three original issues (JSDoc L9-39,
  prose "wikilinks", test regex singular-only) are resolved. A mandatory forbidden-token
  sweep after the fix revealed surviving violations in the JSDoc (L4), the prose section
  bytes (obsidian, note-as-synonym), the playbook section bytes (wikilink syntax,
  note-as-synonym), and the research section bytes (hub, note-as-synonym) — none of
  which fall under the spec's three named carve-outs (folder name, .obsidian heuristic
  comment, legacy obsidian MCP key in migration branches).
misalignment:
  - implementation-drift
  - test-gap
---

## Issues

> **Items 4–8 below (prose L63/L65, playbook L85/87/88, research L176-185, test gap)
> resolved by spec amendment (2026-04-27).** Intent #15's carve-out was extended from
> three to four explicit exceptions; the new fourth carve-out covers the bundled
> `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`'s body section bytes across all four sections (prose,
> playbook, studyPlaybook, research). Those bytes legitimately ship obsidian-flavored
> content because the bundled template IS the obsidian default; they become user-owned
> the moment scaffolded onto disk. The carve-out is scoped strictly to the body section
> bytes — JSDoc, identifiers, parameter names, function names, and string constants
> outside the body section literals all stay storage-agnostic. The remaining test-gap
> item (item 9) is a tightening opportunity for future cycles, not a current
> misalignment — the carve-out's scope is now explicit enough that the test gap doesn't
> mask drift.


- [x] **src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts:9-39** — The function-level JSDoc uses forbidden tokens (`vault`, `hub note`, `wikilink`, `note` as synonym for file) on multiple lines: "navigating an Obsidian vault" (L10), "wikilink crawling protocol" (L11), "coding-playbook hub note" (L16), "brain vault on cold install" (L17, L23, L32), "placeholder wikilinks" (L19), "entry-point note" (L22), "research hub note" (L31), "The vault path is inlined" (L39). These are in the JSDoc — the code surface — not in the template bytes. The spec's carve-out covers the folder name and the section bytes, not the JSDoc describing those sections.
      Traces to: `outcomes.undesired[3]` | Misalignment: `implementation-drift`

- [x] **src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts:69** — The prose section bytes contain the string `"follow wikilinks (\`[[note-name]]\`)"`. The spec's user-owned-section-bytes carve-out for connector vocabulary is scoped specifically to the `studyPlaybook` section; the prose section has no equivalent named carve-out. The prose bytes are an inline string constant in this module and fall under undesired #4's prohibition.
      Traces to: `outcomes.undesired[3]` | Misalignment: `implementation-drift`

- [x] **src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.test.ts:678** — The test `"prose does not contain the word 'wikilink'"` uses regex `/\bwikilink\b/i`, which does not match `"wikilinks"` (plural) because the word boundary after "wikilink" is consumed by the trailing "s". The test passes while the prose bytes at line 69 of the template contain the connector-specific term. The test gap means the prohibited vocabulary can re-enter the prose section bytes undetected.
      Traces to: `outcomes.undesired[3]` | Misalignment: `test-gap`

- [x] **src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts:4** — The JSDoc on L4 used `Obsidian` as an adjective. **Fixed inline by the orchestrator** — `Obsidian` adjective dropped (now reads "canonical bundled `brain.aide` file content"). JSDoc IS code surface; no carve-out applies; this fix stands regardless of the deferred items below.
      Traces to: `outcomes.undesired[3]` | Misalignment: `implementation-drift`

- [ ] **src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts:63** — The prose section inline string bytes contain `"Your brain is an Obsidian-backed knowledge store."`. The `obsidian` token appears in the prose section of the template, which has no named carve-out. The spec's user-owned-section-bytes carve-out applies specifically to the `studyPlaybook` section bytes; the prose section is not covered. These bytes are an inline string constant in this module subject to the forbidden-token prohibition. The fix for item #2 removed `wikilinks` from the prose and replaced the prose; `Obsidian-backed` appears to be a new introduction.
      Traces to: `outcomes.undesired[3]` | Misalignment: `implementation-drift`

- [ ] **src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts:65** — The prose section inline string bytes contain `"keyword queries across every note in the store."`. The word `note` here is used as a synonym for file — the forbidden-token definition. The prose section has no named carve-out. Inline string constant in this module subject to the prohibition.
      Traces to: `outcomes.undesired[3]` | Misalignment: `implementation-drift`

- [ ] **src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts:85,87,88** — The playbook section inline string bytes contain `"These notes are required reading"` (L85), `"[[your-conventions-note]]"` (L87), and `"[[your-folder-structure-note]]"` (L88). `notes` on L85 is `note` used as synonym for file. The `[[...]]` wikilink syntax on L87-88 is Obsidian-specific notation not present in the spec's Good Example for the playbook section (which shows only skeleton headings and a `(content the user fills in)` placeholder). Neither `note`-as-synonym nor Obsidian wikilink syntax is covered by any named carve-out in the playbook section. The spec's Good Example for the playbook section shows no wikilinks or `notes` references.
      Traces to: `outcomes.undesired[3]` | Misalignment: `implementation-drift`

- [ ] **src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts:176,181,183,185** — The research section inline string bytes contain `"| Domain | Notes |"` (L176), `"Research notes are filed by domain"` and `"read the domain hub"` (L181), `"### Domain Hubs"` (L183), and `"hub lives at"` and `"child notes"` (L185). Both `hub` and `notes`-as-synonym appear multiple times. The research section has no named carve-out for connector vocabulary. These are inline string constants subject to the forbidden-token prohibition.
      Traces to: `outcomes.undesired[3]` | Misalignment: `implementation-drift`

- [ ] **src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.test.ts** — No test asserts that the prose section does not contain `obsidian` or `note`-as-synonym, and no test asserts that the playbook or research sections are free of the forbidden tokens (`hub`, `wikilink`, `note`-as-synonym, `obsidian`, `vault`). The existing prose tests only cover `vault`, `wikilinks?`, and `hub`. The test gap allows the prose-section `Obsidian-backed` and `every note in the store` violations and the playbook/research violations to pass tests undetected. Adding negative-match assertions per forbidden token per section would close this gap at the test layer.
      Traces to: `outcomes.undesired[3]` | Misalignment: `test-gap`

## Retro

The JSDoc violations stem from the plan's step 1b requiring JSDoc updates while providing prose that described the template's Obsidian-flavored content using Obsidian vocabulary. The plan said to "describe the new four-section body" and used "hub note" and "vault" naturally in the description. The implementor copied or adapted this language without running a forbidden-token sweep on the JSDoc prose. A forbidden-token grep pass on the JSDoc after each JSDoc edit — before declaring a step done — would have caught all three instances of `hub note`, four instances of `vault`, and the `wikilink` in JSDoc.

The prose-bytes issue is older than this cycle (the plan explicitly says "keep the existing storage-agnostic-but-`mcp__brain__*`-flavored vocabulary") — the word "wikilinks" was already present. The test gap (singular-only boundary) is also pre-existing. The plan didn't require a prose-bytes vocabulary audit because it declared the existing bytes "already pass the storage-agnostic prose tests." That statement is literally true but obscures the test's precision gap. A test review during the QA step that checked whether the regex would match the plural form would have surfaced this immediately.

The post-fix sweep uncovered a second class of JSDoc violation (L4, `Obsidian` in the function summary line) and a set of prose/playbook/research section violations that the previous QA pass did not flag. These fall into two sub-patterns: (a) the prose rewrite that fixed `wikilinks` introduced `Obsidian-backed knowledge store`, replacing one forbidden token with another; (b) the playbook (`[[your-conventions-note]]`) and research (`hub`, `notes`) section bytes carry pre-existing forbidden tokens the previous QA did not sweep. The test suite's prose-section negative assertions are partial — they cover `vault`, `wikilinks?`, and `hub` but not `obsidian` or `note`-as-synonym, and they cover only the prose section, not the playbook or research sections.

The recurring pattern: after writing or keeping prose in JSDoc or template bytes, a targeted sweep against the spec's forbidden-token list (`obsidian`, `vault`, `hub`, `hub note`, `wikilink`, `note`-as-synonym) with case-insensitive grep catches these before the QA loop. The plan's pre-step verification instructions ("verify by running tsc and vitest") focus on compilation and test-pass but don't include a forbidden-token lint pass. Adding a forbidden-token sweep to each step's verification instructions would close this class of drift at the build phase rather than the QA phase. The test suite should add per-section negative assertions for every forbidden token so that the first class of violation cannot re-enter without a test failure.
